WEBVTT

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.701
A dam is a wall that holds back water.

00:00:07.051 --> 00:00:10.441
Here's a pair of them, each
holding water fifty metres deep.

00:00:10.791 --> 00:00:14.970
Behind the top dam is a lake
that runs back ten kilometres.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:20.336
Behind the bottom one is a slot of water
two metres wide, with solid rock beyond it.

00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:22.497
Which dam has to be stronger?

00:00:25.347 --> 00:00:27.065
It's tempting to pick the lake.

00:00:27.415 --> 00:00:31.966
There's so much more water behind it,
and all of it seems to lean on the wall.

00:00:32.316 --> 00:00:35.103
But follow where the lake's
weight actually goes.

00:00:35.453 --> 00:00:38.073
Cut the lake into columns, and each column

00:00:38.073 --> 00:00:40.375
just presses down on
the ground beneath it.

00:00:40.725 --> 00:00:42.815
The lake bed carries the lake.

00:00:43.165 --> 00:00:46.648
Only the water right against
the dam pushes sideways on it.

00:00:46.998 --> 00:00:49.831
So what decides how
hard that water pushes?

00:00:51.081 --> 00:00:54.471
If you've ever dived down off a
beach, you've felt the answer.

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:57.700
A few metres down, your
ears start to hurt.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:02.927
The water above you presses in, and the
deeper you go, the harder it presses.

00:01:03.277 --> 00:01:06.697
Water passes that press on
in every direction, sideways

00:01:06.697 --> 00:01:09.732
too, so it pushes on a wall just as hard.

00:01:10.082 --> 00:01:13.890
Each metre of depth adds about
a tonne on every square metre.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.116
How wide the sea is doesn't matter,
only how far you are below the surface.

00:01:19.466 --> 00:01:22.252
So lower a pressure gauge
to the foot of each dam.

00:01:22.602 --> 00:01:26.503
Both read the same, about fifty
tonnes on every square metre.

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:32.008
The lake behind one dam and the slot behind
the other make no difference at all.

00:01:32.358 --> 00:01:36.282
A Flemish engineer, Simon
Stevin, wrote this down in

00:01:36.282 --> 00:01:39.092
fifteen eighty-six, and it
still surprises people.

00:01:40.342 --> 00:01:43.322
That's the pressure on
one square metre, but the

00:01:43.322 --> 00:01:45.636
water presses on the
whole face of the dam.

00:01:45.986 --> 00:01:48.865
Draw the pressure as arrows down the face.

00:01:49.215 --> 00:01:52.175
At the surface the arrow
has no length at all,

00:01:52.175 --> 00:01:54.417
and it grows steadily toward the bottom.

00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:57.447
Together the arrows fill
a triangle, and the

00:01:57.447 --> 00:02:00.014
area of that triangle is the whole push.

00:02:00.364 --> 00:02:05.241
On every metre along the dam, it comes to
about twelve hundred and fifty tonnes.

00:02:05.591 --> 00:02:07.913
Now make the water a hundred metres deep.

00:02:08.263 --> 00:02:11.763
The triangle is twice as
tall, and twice as wide at the

00:02:11.763 --> 00:02:14.671
bottom, because the pressure
there is twice as big.

00:02:15.021 --> 00:02:18.040
So it holds four copies
of the first triangle.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.873
That's about five thousand
tonnes on every metre of dam.

00:02:22.223 --> 00:02:25.149
Double the depth, and the
push grows four times.

00:02:26.399 --> 00:02:29.139
So the two dams feel
exactly the same push.

00:02:29.489 --> 00:02:32.693
Depth sets the push,
and nothing else does.

00:02:33.043 --> 00:02:35.463
The size of the lake
decides something different:

00:02:35.463 --> 00:02:38.430
how much water gets out if the dam fails.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:44.263
The four-times rule also explains
the shape of a concrete dam.

00:02:44.613 --> 00:02:48.839
Most of the push is low down, so the
wall has to be thick at the bottom.

00:02:49.189 --> 00:02:53.926
Make the wall a triangle, and its own
weight grows the same way the push does.

00:02:54.276 --> 00:02:58.436
Pick any level: the weight of concrete
above it and the push of water

00:02:58.436 --> 00:03:02.449
beside it grow together, so they
stay in step all the way down.

00:03:02.799 --> 00:03:05.419
That's why big concrete
dams that stand by their

00:03:05.419 --> 00:03:07.815
own weight are triangles in cross-section.

00:03:08.165 --> 00:03:10.347
Engineers call them gravity dams.

00:03:11.597 --> 00:03:15.823
But a concrete triangle can't count on
being glued to the rock beneath it.

00:03:16.173 --> 00:03:20.074
Weight and friction hold it in place,
like a block resting on a ramp.

00:03:20.424 --> 00:03:22.864
Friction comes from
pressing: the harder the

00:03:22.864 --> 00:03:25.347
block presses down, the more grip it has.

00:03:25.697 --> 00:03:30.387
Now slip a thin cushion of water under
the block, and pump up its pressure.

00:03:30.737 --> 00:03:33.277
The water carries part of
the block's weight, so

00:03:33.277 --> 00:03:36.125
the block presses less,
and its grip shrinks.

00:03:36.475 --> 00:03:39.540
Nothing about the block
changed, and yet it slides.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:42.710
The grip on offer, divided
by the grip the block

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:45.416
needs, is called the factor of safety.

00:03:45.766 --> 00:03:49.899
Above one, the block holds,
and below one, it slides.

00:03:51.249 --> 00:03:54.314
Is there a cushion like
that under a concrete dam?

00:03:55.564 --> 00:03:59.047
Go back to the beach and dig
a hole near the water's edge.

00:03:59.397 --> 00:04:01.719
It fills with water, from below.

00:04:02.069 --> 00:04:06.189
The water between the sand grains
is connected to the sea, so

00:04:06.189 --> 00:04:09.221
at each depth it has the pressure
the sea would have there.

00:04:09.571 --> 00:04:14.819
The rock under a dam is cracked, and
water gets into the cracks the same way.

00:04:15.169 --> 00:04:18.309
Here's a concrete dam drawn
to scale, sixty metres

00:04:18.309 --> 00:04:20.648
tall, with the water nearly to its top.

00:04:20.998 --> 00:04:23.367
Water seeps in under its base.

00:04:23.717 --> 00:04:28.036
At the upstream edge it pushes up with
the full pressure of the reservoir.

00:04:28.386 --> 00:04:33.262
At the downstream edge it pushes up only as
hard as the shallow river below the dam.

00:04:33.612 --> 00:04:38.488
In between, the push falls in a straight
line, so it makes a wedge under the base.

00:04:38.838 --> 00:04:41.532
That push from below is called uplift.

00:04:41.882 --> 00:04:45.132
It's tempting to think a dam
this heavy wouldn't notice.

00:04:45.482 --> 00:04:48.622
But concrete is less than
two and a half times as heavy

00:04:48.622 --> 00:04:51.845
as water, and the water
pushes on the whole base.

00:04:53.095 --> 00:04:54.720
So designers bleed it off.

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:59.575
From a tunnel inside the dam, they drill
a line of holes down into the rock.

00:04:59.925 --> 00:05:02.885
Each hole is under ten
centimetres across, and they're

00:05:02.885 --> 00:05:05.265
spaced a few metres apart along the dam.

00:05:05.615 --> 00:05:07.334
Engineers call them drains.

00:05:07.684 --> 00:05:10.804
Water that reaches the
holes escapes up them, so

00:05:10.804 --> 00:05:13.396
the pressure under the
base drops sharply there.

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:18.126
A rule designers often start from is
that, at the drains, the pressure

00:05:18.126 --> 00:05:22.105
falls two thirds of the way from
the reservoir's down to the river's.

00:05:22.455 --> 00:05:26.727
With the drains working, the uplift cancels
about a quarter of the dam's weight.

00:05:27.077 --> 00:05:29.028
Now check whether it slides.

00:05:29.378 --> 00:05:31.958
The grip is the weight
still pressing on the rock,

00:05:31.958 --> 00:05:34.579
times a friction factor
for concrete on rock.

00:05:34.929 --> 00:05:37.889
Divide the grip by the
water's push, and you get the

00:05:37.889 --> 00:05:40.873
dam's factor of safety,
just like the block's.

00:05:41.223 --> 00:05:43.360
For this dam it's about one point seven.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.990
A common target for
this check is one and a

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.957
half, so the dam passes
with room to spare.

00:05:50.207 --> 00:05:54.480
But drains clog over the years, and
they have to be watched and cleaned.

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:57.850
Block them, and the pressure
under the base runs straight

00:05:57.850 --> 00:06:00.217
from the reservoir's down to the river's.

00:06:00.567 --> 00:06:02.332
The uplift nearly doubles.

00:06:02.682 --> 00:06:05.700
Now it cancels about two
fifths of the dam's weight.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:09.069
The factor of safety falls
to about one point three.

00:06:09.419 --> 00:06:13.970
The dam still stands, but it has lost
the margin its designers asked for.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:15.899
The concrete didn't change.

00:06:16.249 --> 00:06:17.781
The water under it did.

00:06:19.131 --> 00:06:22.631
A concrete dam is the block
on the cushion, and its

00:06:22.631 --> 00:06:25.354
grip is the weight still
pressing on the rock.

00:06:25.704 --> 00:06:29.244
It's the same idea that holds
sand together: the grip comes

00:06:29.244 --> 00:06:32.624
from pressing, and water pressure
in between takes it away.

00:06:34.974 --> 00:06:37.946
Some of these lessons were
learned from dams that failed.

00:06:38.296 --> 00:06:42.940
At Bouzey, in eastern France, a
masonry dam held water for a canal.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:47.284
On its first filling, part of the
wall slid on its leaky sandstone.

00:06:47.634 --> 00:06:50.885
It was propped up with extra
masonry and filled again.

00:06:51.235 --> 00:06:54.355
Then, in April eighteen
ninety-five, it broke, through

00:06:54.355 --> 00:06:57.040
the masonry itself, well
above its foundation.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:59.294
More than eighty people drowned.

00:07:01.144 --> 00:07:03.605
Experts disagreed about why it broke.

00:07:03.955 --> 00:07:08.395
That summer, without naming Bouzey, the
engineer Maurice Lévy told the French

00:07:08.395 --> 00:07:12.500
Academy of Sciences what he saw as
the special danger of high dams.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:16.450
Water forced into cracks
pushes from inside, and he

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:18.748
compared it to a boiler
without a safety valve.

00:07:19.098 --> 00:07:22.478
The rule of the day said the
weight and the water's push,

00:07:22.478 --> 00:07:26.110
taken together, must land in
the middle third of the wall.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:31.104
That had to hold at every level, so
the upstream edge stays pressed down.

00:07:31.454 --> 00:07:33.173
Lévy said that wasn't enough.

00:07:33.523 --> 00:07:38.303
At every level, the masonry at the upstream
face must press down harder than the

00:07:38.303 --> 00:07:42.718
water pushes there, so any water that
gets into a crack is squeezed back out.

00:07:43.068 --> 00:07:45.715
Our dam passes his test all the way up.

00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:49.905
A test like his, with an
allowance for drains, is in the US

00:07:49.905 --> 00:07:52.845
Bureau of Reclamation's design
manual for gravity dams.

00:07:53.195 --> 00:07:56.755
Failures like Bouzey led
engineers to build gravity

00:07:56.755 --> 00:07:59.650
dams thicker, and to drain
their foundations deeper.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:05.265
Slot or lake, the push is the
same, and so is the uplift.

00:08:05.615 --> 00:08:08.263
Water can't soak through
the concrete itself.

00:08:08.613 --> 00:08:12.142
But most of the world's big
dams aren't concrete at all.

00:08:12.492 --> 00:08:15.743
They're heaps of earth and rock,
and water passes through soil.

00:08:16.093 --> 00:08:19.483
So how can a wall of
dirt hold back a lake?
